een the two sides on the events that transpired. I think that=E2=80=99s imp=
ortant. It wasn=E2=80=99t just one thing on one side or the other that brou=
ght the talks down; there were real differences in priorities and values th=
at couldn=E2=80=99t get resolved.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25=
px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700=
">What did that look like=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px ge=
orgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The French-language issue is =
an interesting example. French is an incredibly emotional and foundational =
thing for Canada. Policies that promote and protect the use of the French l=
anguage are the cornerstone of keeping the country together with Quebec. Ca=
nada actually has a law that compels streaming platforms to place French-la=
nguage content more prominently for Canadian subscribers. The U.S. was pres=
sing Canada to repeal that law.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px=
 georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>So, to Canadian ears, when=
 the U.S. side said, We don=E2=80=99t want to tell Netflix to promote Quebe=
cois shows, they heard, =E2=80=9CYou=E2=80=99re trying to undermine our pol=
icy of promoting and protecting the French language.=E2=80=9D The U.S. nego=
tiators weren=E2=80=99t necessarily thinking that way =E2=80=94 they=E2=80=
=99re thinking: =E2=80=9CWe don=E2=80=99t have a problem with the French la=
nguage. We=E2=80=99re just telling you that we don=E2=80=99t want to tell A=
merican platforms what to do.=E2=80=9D Now, could they have resolved this w=
ith more time=3F Possibly. But that=E2=80=99s where politics and deadlines =
came into play. The deadline arrived, Mark Carney looked at the deal, didn=
=E2=80=99t like what he saw, and said, We=E2=80=99re not doing it.</span></=
